{
    "title": "Cost of Living Adjustment to Public Broadcasters Compulsory License Royalty Rate",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "November 28, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-11-28/pdf/2025-21579.pdf",
    "summary": "Here are three quiet but meaningful federal updates: a housekeeping fix to mining cleanup rules, a small 2026 royalty increase for college and other noncommercial radio stations, and federal approval of Montana\u2019s plan to cut haze and improve views in national parks and wilderness areas.\n\nFirst, the Interior Department cleaned up an old regulation tied to noncoal mine land reclamation. The change removes outdated language and clarifies that uncertified states and tribes can\u2019t tap federal mine cleanup funds to fix uranium mill tailings sites or Superfund sites, which are handled under other programs. The department says this won\u2019t materially affect tribes, energy supply, the environment, or paperwork\u2014it\u2019s largely a clarity update.\n\nSecond, the Copyright Royalty Board set a cost\u2011of\u2011living adjustment for 2026 that nudges the annual fee paid by noncommercial radio stations at colleges, universities, and similar schools for using music from two licensing groups up from $199 to $205 per station. This reflects inflation over the past year and keeps the rate in line with everyday costs.\n\nFinally, the Environmental Protection Agency approved Montana\u2019s plan for the next phase of the national effort to reduce haze. The goal is clearer views at protected parks and wilderness areas by cutting pollution from fine particles and related gases. EPA also signed off on a related visibility piece of Montana\u2019s broader air quality plan.\n\nWho benefits? Clearer rules help state agencies and tribes avoid confusion about which funds can be used for which cleanups. Songwriters and music rights holders see a modest boost from the radio fee update, while schools get certainty about what they\u2019ll pay in 2026. In Montana and downwind states, residents and visitors should gain from cleaner air and better vistas in treasured public lands.\n\nWho might feel downsides? College and noncommercial stations\u2014often on tight budgets\u2014will pay a bit more, even if the increase is small. In Montana, facilities covered by the state\u2019s haze plan may face added costs to meet the pollution goals. For the mining rule cleanup, impacts appear minimal, as it mainly removes obsolete language rather than changing policy. Overall, these are incremental shifts aimed at clarity, inflation adjustments, and steady progress on cleaner air.",
